<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Local Farm Stand – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How Much Are the Bananas?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about people or things using simple language. Interactive: Role-play simple social interactions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about buying and selling things in familiar places (e.g., "Do you have any apples? I want to buy two." "Yes, I have some."). Can role-play simple social interactions using polite language (e.g., "Can I have two mangos, please?"). Can ask questions about prices during role-play (e.g., "How much is this apple?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Farm Stand Inventory" activity. The teacher shows flashcards of fruits and vegetables (e.g., banana, mango, pineapple, tomato, lettuce) introduced in previous lessons. Students chorally repeat the names. The teacher then says, "I want to buy some..." and students call out the item. This activates vocabulary and sets the theme for buying and selling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, e.g., a "banana," and says slowly and clearly: "Banana. How much are the bananas?" Then holds up "papaya" and says, "Papaya. Do you have any papayas?" The teacher models the polite request "Can I have some bananas, please?" emphasizing clear pronunciation of /p/ in "papaya" and /b/ in "banana," and guides students to segment "banana" as "ba-na-na" by clapping syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I asking to buy something or to eat something?" (Expected answer: To buy something.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'please,' is it polite or not polite?" (Expected answer: Polite.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue script displayed on the board or projected, featuring a customer and a vendor at a farm stand. The dialogue incorporates the target grammar (WH-questions, polite requests, some/any) and vocabulary (mango, banana, price, buy, sell). For instance:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Customer:</b> "Hello! Do you have any mangos, please?"<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> "Yes, I have some fresh mangos. How many do you want?"<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> "I want two mangos, please. And... do you have any bananas?"<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> "Yes, I have a lot of ripe bananas. How much are they?"<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> "How much is one banana?"<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> "One banana is fifty cents."<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> "Okay, can I have three bananas, please?"<br>
                <b>Vendor:</b> "Of course! Two mangos and three bananas. That's two dollars, please."<br>
                <b>Customer:</b> "Thank you!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engages in simple, polite exchanges to acquire goods or services in a familiar transactional context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice, modeling intonation and rhythm.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Where are the people?" (At the farm stand/market) "Are they friends or buying things?" (Buying things)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The customer wants five bananas." (False) "The vendor has mangos." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing polite requests ("Can I have...?", "Do you have any...?"), WH-questions ("How much is...?", "How many...?"), and using "some" and "any" with countable nouns (e.g., mango, banana, pineapple). Vocabulary: mango, pineapple, guava, tomato, pepper, lettuce, papaya, banana, fruit, vegetable, price. Pronunciation: /p/ and /b/ sounds, syllable segmentation for words like "banana" (ba-na-na), "papaya" (pa-pa-ya).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Dialogue Repetition and Cloze:</b> Students repeat lines from the dialogue chorally and individually. Then, the teacher erases key words (e.g., fruit names, "some," "any," "how much") from the projected dialogue, and students fill in the blanks orally, practicing sentence completion in pairs. E.g., "Do you have ____ apples?" (any) "I want ____ water." (some).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill ("What do you have?"):</b> The teacher displays a variety of fruit and vegetable flashcards with prices (e.g., banana $0.50, pineapple $2.00, guava $1.00). The teacher says, "I am the customer." Then points to a flashcard (e.g., 'papaya') and models: "Do you have any papayas? How much is a papaya?" Students then take turns asking and answering using different flashcards. Teacher guides students to differentiate between "How much is (singular)?" and "How much are (plural)?".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction ("Ask and Answer"):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of different fruit/vegetable flashcards with imaginary prices. Student A asks about two items, and Student B answers. They must use "Do you have any...?" "Yes, I have some..." / "No, I don't have any..." and "How much is/are...?" Then they switch roles. Teacher circulates, providing corrective feedback on pronunciation and grammar, especially for /p/, /b/ and syllable count.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Role-play ("Our Farm Stand").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs or small groups (3 students: 1 vendor, 2 customers). They set up a mini "Farm Stand" using their flashcards/drawings and pretend money. They are instructed to use the target language to buy and sell at least three different items, incorporating polite requests and asking about prices and availability. The scenario: Customers visit the farm stand and must buy some fruits and vegetables for a healthy snack. They must use the polite expressions learned (e.g., "Can I have two mangos, please?"). The vendor must respond with availability and prices, using "some/any" (e.g., "Yes, I have some delicious mangos. Two mangos are one dollar."). Students are encouraged to use /p/ and /b/ words, and to count syllables when they say the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This activity focuses on fluency, collaborative interaction, and successfully completing the transactional task of buying and selling items.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play. The teacher walks around, listening to pairs and noting their use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Our Farm Stand" role-play for the teacher or another pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of vocabulary, especially /p/ and /b/ sounds (e.g., papaya, banana, pineapple, pepper).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (fruits, vegetables, price, buy, sell).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of polite requests ("Can I have...?", "please").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of "How much/many...?" and "some/any".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and comprehensibility of the interaction.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and turn-taking in the role-play.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick oral or written exit ticket before leaving class.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you bought today at the farm stand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one polite thing you said?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you to say today when buying food?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and what can you practice more?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The role-play activity worked well to encourage interactive speaking. Students were able to use "How much...?" and polite requests with varying degrees of accuracy. Some students still confused "some" and "any" or struggled with prices. The pronunciation practice of /p/ and /b/ was good, but segmenting syllables needs more reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for sentence construction in writing, building on the speaking practice by having students write simple sentences about their purchases and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the vocabulary and structures practiced today. Students will use the phrases "I want to buy some..." and "How much is/are...?" to write short dialogues or descriptions of items they bought at the farm stand.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>